	"crit_lugeon": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Pocket Critters cards contain data about the creature's type,and where it came from, attacks, ability, and HP as well as any special effects the cards have on the game."], ["ORIGIN", "In the US, it was first published by Lizards of the Coast, towards the end of 1998 to capitalize on the US popularity of Pocket Critters."], ["IN THE GAME", "Afterward, there will be a cut off the top record-holders (approximately the top 1/8 of participants) where players will play best two out of three matches and the loser gets eliminated (standard tournament bracket style), with an eventual winner."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Rarities can be also account for old card collectibles such as \"1st-edition Base Set\" and \"First Generation Sets\", and such collectibles are some of the rarest and most expensive Pocket Critters cards with some valuing at thousands and millions of dollars."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Hasbroo bought Lizards of the Coast in September 1999 for $325 million dollars based on the strength of the Pocket Critters license."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Lizards sued Funtendo on October 1, 2003, and accused the company of poaching employees and violating its patent; the lawsuit was settled out of court."]], "year": 1996},
	"crit_ancient_mewl": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Shigeki Morimotoo"], ["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Psychic"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mewl is one of the many fictional species in the Pocket Critters franchise. It is a small, pink, Psychic-type Mythical Pocket Critters, which are incredibly rare and powerful Pocket Critters typically available only via special events."], ["ORIGIN", "During initial distributions for Mewl, the pool of those who would potentially receive one was relatively small—numbering only twenty in the first and one hundred in the second—due to the fact that each Mewl had to be individually generated on Morimotoo's PC and then manually traded to winning cartridges."], ["IN THE GAME", "At the time of its introduction, Mewl could only be legitimately obtained in the Pocket Critters video games via Funtendo promotional event distributions."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2021, Oreos depicting Pocket Critters characters were released, with those depicting Mewl being particularly rare and sought after by fans of the series, leading to bids on sales of the cookie jumping up to prices as high as several thousand dollars."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Pocket Critters Sun and Moon, a special \"Z-Crystal\" item was created for Mewl, which allowed it to use a powerful attack only it could use, known as \"Genesys Supernova\"."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_gloomeon_vmax_alternate_art": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Counters are stacked on top of cards in play to indicate damage dealt, and players may choose to put their Pocket Critters cards on a bench to switch to a different attacker."], ["ORIGIN", "Lizards sued Funtendo on October 1, 2003, and accused the company of poaching employees and violating its patent; the lawsuit was settled out of court."], ["IN THE GAME", "The first Pocket Critters TCG tournament began on June 14–15, 1997, at the Makuhari Messe Event Hall. The player going first cannot attack or play a Supporter card (powerful Trainer effects card) on their first turn."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2016, it was the year's top-selling toy in the strategic card game subclass. As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The \"Pikapal Illustrator\" is the rarest and most expensive Pocket Critters card ever sold in history, and it was acquired by the celebrity and collector Logan Paul for $5,275,000 in July 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Created as a prize for the 1997-1998 Pocket Critters design contests organized by the Japanese manga-magazine KoroKoro Comic and with only 41 copies printed, it is the only Pocket Critters card to say \"Illustrator\" instead of \"Trainer\" like other Trainer cards."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Notable references include Austin Brewen who won the first junior tournament, Brenden Zhang who won the first Senior Tournament, and Arturo Heras who won the first Master Tournament."]], "year": 1996},
	"crit_master_s_key": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Standard gameplay cards include Pocket Critters cards, energy cards, trainer cards, and stadium cards, a variant of trainer cards."], ["ORIGIN", "In the US, it was first published by Lizards of the Coast, towards the end of 1998 to capitalize on the US popularity of Pocket Critters."], ["IN THE GAME", "The top three player of the tournament were awarded the No.1, 2, and 3 trainer trophy cards; this practice continue through subsequent Pocket Critters TCG tournaments and organized plays."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2017, it had an 82% share of Europe's strategic card game market. As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Rarities can be also account for old card collectibles such as \"1st-edition Base Set\" and \"First Generation Sets\", and such collectibles are some of the rarest and most expensive Pocket Critters cards with some valuing at thousands and millions of dollars."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Created as a prize for the 1997-1998 Pocket Critters design contests organized by the Japanese manga-magazine KoroKoro Comic and with only 41 copies printed, it is the only Pocket Critters card to say \"Illustrator\" instead of \"Trainer\" like other Trainer cards."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Notable references include Austin Brewen who won the first junior tournament, Brenden Zhang who won the first Senior Tournament, and Arturo Heras who won the first Master Tournament."]], "year": 1996},
	"crit_gold_star_mewl": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Shigeki Morimotoo"], ["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Psychic"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mewl is one of the many fictional species in the Pocket Critters franchise. It is a small, pink, Psychic-type Mythical Pocket Critters, which are incredibly rare and powerful Pocket Critters typically available only via special events."], ["ORIGIN", "During initial distributions for Mewl, the pool of those who would potentially receive one was relatively small—numbering only twenty in the first and one hundred in the second—due to the fact that each Mewl had to be individually generated on Morimotoo's PC and then manually traded to winning cartridges."], ["IN THE GAME", "At the time of its introduction, Mewl could only be legitimately obtained in the Pocket Critters video games via Funtendo promotional event distributions."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2021, Oreos depicting Pocket Critters characters were released, with those depicting Mewl being particularly rare and sought after by fans of the series, leading to bids on sales of the cookie jumping up to prices as high as several thousand dollars."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In Pocket Critters Sun and Moon, a special \"Z-Crystal\" item was created for Mewl, which allowed it to use a powerful attack only it could use, known as \"Genesys Supernova\"."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_gold_star_charblaze": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Fire and Flying Fire and Dragon (Mega Charblaze X)"], ["DESIGNER", "Tomohiro Kitakaze"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Charblaze, known as Lizardon in Japan, is a Pocket Critters in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise. Created by Atsuko Nishida, Charblaze first appeared in the video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue (Pocket Critters Red and Green in Japan) and subsequent sequels."], ["ORIGIN", "Whereas its pre-evolutions Charmandoo and Charmeleon are ground-bound lizard like creatures, Charblaze's design is inspired by dragons, more specifically European dragons."], ["IN THE GAME", "It is available only through evolving Charmandoo, who is one of the first Pocket Critters the player can choose at the start of the game."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2005, search engine Yahoo! reported Charblaze as \"one of the top Pocket Critters-related web searches \", while in 2020, Charblaze was voted as one of the top ten most popular Pocket Critters by fans."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cards featuring the character have been stated to be the most desired of the series, quickly rising to high prices amongst collectors and retailers."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A street in Las Vegas was named after Charblaze, while a species of bee, Chillicola charizard, was named after the species."]], "year": 0},
