	"crit_lugeon_v_alternate_art": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The game was originally released in Japan by Media Factory in 1996 as Pocket Monsters, which was based on the Pocket Critters Red, Blue, and Yellow PocketBoy video games."], ["ORIGIN", "In the US, it was first published by Lizards of the Coast, towards the end of 1998 to capitalize on the US popularity of Pocket Critters."], ["IN THE GAME", "The first Pocket Critters TCG tournament began on June 14–15, 1997, at the Makuhari Messe Event Hall. The player going first cannot attack or play a Supporter card (powerful Trainer effects card) on their first turn."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2017, it had an 82% share of Europe's strategic card game market. As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Hasbroo bought Lizards of the Coast in September 1999 for $325 million dollars based on the strength of the Pocket Critters license."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Shopping platform ZenPlus, a subsidiary of shopping service ZenMarket (now part of ZenGroup), sold two of the cards in 2020, for the Japanese Yen equivalent of US$233,000 and US$208,496 respectively."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Lizards sued Funtendo on October 1, 2003, and accused the company of poaching employees and violating its patent; the lawsuit was settled out of court."]], "year": 1996},
	"crit_pikapal_with_grey_felt_hat": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Atsuko Nizhida"], ["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Electric"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Originally designed to be the first part of a three-stage evolution line in Red and Blue, Pikapal evolves into Raichoo through the use of a \"Thunder Stone\" item, with Raichoo intended to be able to evolve into 'Gorochu'."], ["ORIGIN", "Originally after evolving into Raichoo, Raichoo was then going to evolve into a Pocket Critters called \"Gorochu.\" Gorochu was cut due to cartridge space concerns, leaving Raichoo to be Pikapal's sole evolution through use of the game's \"Thunder Stone\" item."], ["IN THE GAME", "Outside of the main series, Pikapal stars in Hey You, Pikapal! for the Funtendo 64; the player interacts with Pikapal through a microphone, issuing commands to play various mini-games and act out situations."], ["RECEPTION", "The name was inspired due to Pikapal's \"lightning-fast moves and shocking electric effects\". In 2024, a poll conducted by BAFTA with around 4,000 respondents named Pikapal as the twelfth most iconic video-game character of all time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One of these collectible cards was \"Pikapal Illustrator\", limited to about 20-40 printed in 1998, and was auctioned off for about $55,000 in 2016, and then $375,000 in 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Another card, sold in 2022, went for nearly a million dollars."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Pikapal has been well-received by reviewers; it was ranked as the \"second best person of the year\" by Time in 1999, who called it \"the most beloved animated character since Hello Kitteh \"."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_blane_s_charblaze": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Fire and Flying Fire and Dragon (Mega Charblaze X)"], ["DESIGNER", "Tomohiro Kitakaze"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Charblaze, known as Lizardon in Japan, is a Pocket Critters in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise. Created by Atsuko Nishida, Charblaze first appeared in the video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue (Pocket Critters Red and Green in Japan) and subsequent sequels."], ["ORIGIN", "Whereas its pre-evolutions Charmandoo and Charmeleon are ground-bound lizard like creatures, Charblaze's design is inspired by dragons, more specifically European dragons."], ["IN THE GAME", "It is available only through evolving Charmandoo, who is one of the first Pocket Critters the player can choose at the start of the game."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2005, search engine Yahoo! reported Charblaze as \"one of the top Pocket Critters-related web searches \", while in 2020, Charblaze was voted as one of the top ten most popular Pocket Critters by fans."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cards featuring the character have been stated to be the most desired of the series, quickly rising to high prices amongst collectors and retailers."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A street in Las Vegas was named after Charblaze, while a species of bee, Chillicola charizard, was named after the species."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_ho_hum": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Basic (unevolved) Pocket Critters can change into their evolved forms during the game by playing subsequent evolution cards in the deck."], ["ORIGIN", "Lizards sued Funtendo on October 1, 2003, and accused the company of poaching employees and violating its patent; the lawsuit was settled out of court."], ["IN THE GAME", "The first version of Pocket Critters Play It! was released in 1999, followed by its sequel Pocket Critters Play It! The first Pocket Critters TCG tournament began on June 14–15, 1997, at the Makuhari Messe Event Hall."], ["RECEPTION", "As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Hasbroo bought Lizards of the Coast in September 1999 for $325 million dollars based on the strength of the Pocket Critters license."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Created as a prize for the 1997-1998 Pocket Critters design contests organized by the Japanese manga-magazine KoroKoro Comic and with only 41 copies printed, it is the only Pocket Critters card to say \"Illustrator\" instead of \"Trainer\" like other Trainer cards."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Notable references include Austin Brewen who won the first junior tournament, Brenden Zhang who won the first Senior Tournament, and Arturo Heras who won the first Master Tournament."]], "year": 1996},
	"crit_gold_star_pikapal": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Atsuko Nizhida"], ["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Electric"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pikapal is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters media franchise, and the franchise's mascot."], ["ORIGIN", "Developer Junichi Masuda noted Pikapal's name as one of the most difficult to create, due to an effort of wanting to make it appealing to both Japanese and American audiences."], ["IN THE GAME", "The game Pocket Critters Yellow features a Pikapal, which is given to the player as their first Pocket Critters."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2024, a poll conducted by BAFTA with around 4,000 respondents named Pikapal as the twelfth most iconic video-game character of all time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One of these collectible cards was \"Pikapal Illustrator\", limited to about 20-40 printed in 1998, and was auctioned off for about $55,000 in 2016, and then $375,000 in 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2021, the first Pocket Critters Jet (Boeing 747-400D) featuring entirely Pikapal debuted."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Originally designed to be the first part of a three-stage evolution line in Red and Blue, Pikapal evolves into Raichoo through the use of a \"Thunder Stone\" item, with Raichoo intended to be able to evolve into 'Gorochu'."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_gengrin": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Ghost and Poison"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gengrin is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters media franchise."], ["ORIGIN", "Standing 4 ft 11 in (150 cm) tall, Gengrin has a round purple body, short arms and legs, two red eyes, and a perpetual toothy grin."], ["IN THE GAME", "Gengrin has additionally appeared in the Pocket Critters anime, most notably as a Pocket Critters used by series protagonist Ash Ketchum."], ["RECEPTION", "Described as \"the most famous of the Ghost-type Pocket Critters\" by GamesRadar+, Gengrin has been well received, placing 10th on Critter Co.'s Pocket Critters of the Year poll which featured all Pocket Critters from throughout the franchise."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_dragonut": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Pocket Critters cards contain data about the creature's type,and where it came from, attacks, ability, and HP as well as any special effects the cards have on the game."], ["ORIGIN", "In the US, it was first published by Lizards of the Coast, towards the end of 1998 to capitalize on the US popularity of Pocket Critters."], ["IN THE GAME", "Players in a tournament are split into three age categories: Junior (11 years old and younger), Senior (12 to 15 years old), and Master (16 years old and older)."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2017, it had an 82% share of Europe's strategic card game market. As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Shopping platform ZenPlus, a subsidiary of shopping service ZenMarket (now part of ZenGroup), sold two of the cards in 2020, for the Japanese Yen equivalent of US$233,000 and US$208,496 respectively."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Hasbroo bought Lizards of the Coast in September 1999 for $325 million dollars based on the strength of the Pocket Critters license."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Lizards sued Funtendo on October 1, 2003, and accused the company of poaching employees and violating its patent; the lawsuit was settled out of court."]], "year": 1996},
