	"crit_kabutoptop": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Collectible"], ["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "2–120 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "1996-10-20"], ["CARDS", "60"], ["AGES", "6+"], ["DEVELOPER", "Creatures Inc"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Standard gameplay cards include Pocket Critters cards, energy cards, trainer cards, and stadium cards, a variant of trainer cards."], ["ORIGIN", "Lizards sued Funtendo on October 1, 2003, and accused the company of poaching employees and violating its patent; the lawsuit was settled out of court."], ["IN THE GAME", "The first version of Pocket Critters Play It! was released in 1999, followed by its sequel Pocket Critters Play It! The first Pocket Critters TCG tournament began on June 14–15, 1997, at the Makuhari Messe Event Hall."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2016, it was the year's top-selling toy in the strategic card game subclass. As of March 2023, the game has sold over 52.9 billion cards worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The \"Pikapal Illustrator\" is the rarest and most expensive Pocket Critters card ever sold in history, and it was acquired by the celebrity and collector Logan Paul for $5,275,000 in July 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Shopping platform ZenPlus, a subsidiary of shopping service ZenMarket (now part of ZenGroup), sold two of the cards in 2020, for the Japanese Yen equivalent of US$233,000 and US$208,496 respectively."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Hasbroo bought Lizards of the Coast in September 1999 for $325 million dollars based on the strength of the Pocket Critters license."]], "year": 1996},
	"crit_sabreena_s_gengrin": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Ghost and Poison"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "First introduced in the video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue, it was created by Ken Sugimori, and have appeared in multiple games including Pocket Critters GO and the Pocket Critters Trading Card Gamble, as well as various merchandise related to the franchise."], ["ORIGIN", "Standing 4 ft 11 in (150 cm) tall, Gengrin has a round purple body, short arms and legs, two red eyes, and a perpetual toothy grin."], ["IN THE GAME", "Gengrin has additionally appeared in the Pocket Critters anime, most notably as a Pocket Critters used by series protagonist Ash Ketchum."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2023, Centennial Media's The Ultimate Guide to Pocket Critters noted that after compiling monthly search histories of Google, Yahoo and Microsloth Bing, Gengrin was the fifth highest searched Pocket Critters among internet users with approximately 113,500 average searches per month."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Described as \"the most famous of the Ghost-type Pocket Critters\" by GamesRadar+, Gengrin has been well received, placing 10th on Critter Co.'s Pocket Critters of the Year poll which featured all Pocket Critters from throughout the franchise."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_pikapal": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Atsuko Nizhida"], ["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Electric"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pikapal is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters media franchise, and the franchise's mascot."], ["ORIGIN", "Developer Junichi Masuda noted Pikapal's name as one of the most difficult to create, due to an effort of wanting to make it appealing to both Japanese and American audiences."], ["IN THE GAME", "The game Pocket Critters Yellow features a Pikapal, which is given to the player as their first Pocket Critters."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2024, a poll conducted by BAFTA with around 4,000 respondents named Pikapal as the twelfth most iconic video-game character of all time."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One of these collectible cards was \"Pikapal Illustrator\", limited to about 20-40 printed in 1998, and was auctioned off for about $55,000 in 2016, and then $375,000 in 2021."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2021, the first Pocket Critters Jet (Boeing 747-400D) featuring entirely Pikapal debuted."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Originally designed to be the first part of a three-stage evolution line in Red and Blue, Pikapal evolves into Raichoo through the use of a \"Thunder Stone\" item, with Raichoo intended to be able to evolve into 'Gorochu'."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_bulbasoar": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Grass and Poison"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bulbasoar, known as Fushigidane in Japan, is a fictional Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise."], ["ORIGIN", "Bulbasoar first appeared as one of three Pocket Critters the player could choose as their first at the beginning of the initial PocketBoy games, Pocket Critters Red and Blue, released in Japan in 1996."], ["IN THE GAME", "In making the film, a bulldog was used to understand the top-heavy movement of Bulbasoar, then puppeteers in London were hired to create 3D puppets of it."], ["RECEPTION", "A Bulbasoar statue was installed on March 25, 2018, in the municipality of Suzano, São Paulo, Brazil, and was the first of several other Pocket Critters-related statues installed."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2021, Seiko made limited edition luxury watches based on Bulbasoar and its evolutionary family."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_charmandoo": {"stats": [["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Charmandoo, known as in Japan, is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo's and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise."], ["ORIGIN", "Noticeably, the Charmandoo artwork made by Nishida for the Trading Card Gamble in 2018 showed Charmandoo as having just three fingers on each hand."], ["IN THE GAME", "Ash's Charmandoo originally belonged to a trainer named Damian, who believed it was weak and cruelly abandoned it, telling it to stay on a rocky outcrop until he \"returned.\" The Pocket Critters was very loyal to its trainer and risked its life sitting in the rain, waiting for a trainer who'd never come back to it."], ["RECEPTION", "Michael Derosa of Screen Rant ranked Charmandoo as ninth of the most iconic Pocket Critters from Generation I."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_squirtell": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Water"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Squirtell, known as Zenigame in Japan, is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise."], ["IN THE GAME", "The first video game appearance of Squirtell was in Pocket Critters Red and Blue, alongside the other two starters Charmandoo and Bulbasoar."], ["RECEPTION", "ITmedia also ranked Squirtell as the fourth best first partner Pocket Critters, primarily due to its familiarity even with those not too closely associated with the franchise."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_eevy": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Normal"], ["DESIGNER", "Motofumi Fujiwara"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Eevy is a Pocket Critters species in the Pocket Critters franchise. Created by Satoshi Tajiri and designed by Motofumi Fujiwara, it first appeared in the video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue."], ["IN THE GAME", "The transformation allowed it access to a unique attack known as G-Max Cuddle, which deals damage and infatuates opponents of the opposite gender."], ["RECEPTION", "Japanese fans of the series have coined November 21 as \"Eevy Day\" due the pronunciation being similar to Eevy's Japanese name Eievui."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_psyquack": {"stats": [["FIRST GAME", "Pocket Critters Red and Blue (1996)"], ["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"], ["SPECIES", "Pocket Critters"], ["TYPE", "Water"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Psyquack, known as Koduck (コダック, Kodakku) in Japan, is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise. Created by Ken Sugimori, Psyquack first appeared in the video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue and later in sequels."], ["ORIGIN", "Introduced in Red and Blue, the design started as pixel art sprites by the development team first, with a single color identity chosen to work within the Super PocketBoy hardware limitations."], ["IN THE GAME", "Psyquack appears in the 2019 film Pocket Critters Detective Pikapal as the partner to the female lead of the film, junior unpaid reporter Lucy Stevens (portrayed by Kathryn Newton)."], ["RECEPTION", "The first Pocket Critters Jet, a Boeing 747-400D for All Nippon Airways, has Psyquack and nine other Pocket Critters on its livery."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_magicarp": {"stats": [["FIRST SEEN", "first generation of Pocket Critters"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Magicarp, known in Japan as, is a Pocket Critters species in Funtendo and Game Freek's Pocket Critters franchise. Created by Ken Sugimori, Magicarp first appeared in the video games Pocket Critters Red and Blue and subsequent sequels."], ["IN THE GAME", "In the anime, Magicarp has appeared several times, most notably as the subject of a running gag in which a salesman attempts to trick Team Rockit into buying Magicarp in various guises, the first one being in Battle Aboard the St."], ["RECEPTION", "Steven Bogos of The Escapist listed Magicarp as 61st of their favorite Pocket Critters, stating that one of the most useless Pocket Critters, that if you can manage to level up, turns into one of the most bad-ass: Gyaradoze."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "GamesRadar described it as \"the ultimate in useless Pocket Critters\", though noted its evolution Gyaradoze as one of the \"most well-known\" characters in the series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In the first generation of Pocket Critters games, Magicarp can only learn 2 moves by level."], ["ONE MORE THING", "From generations 2 to 8, Magicarp can learn the aforementioned moves at those respective levels, plus the move Flail, at level 25."]], "year": 0},
	"crit_base_set_booster_pack": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "In collectible card games, digital collectible card games and collectible miniature wargames, a booster pack is a sealed package of cards or figurines, designed to add to a player's collection. A box of multiple booster packs is referred to as a booster box."], ["RECEPTION", "In Limited Edition Alpha and Limited Edition Beta, as well as from Unlimited Edition onward, the cards were sold in 15 cards per booster: 11 commons, 1 rare, and 3 uncommons; Core Set boosters contain a basic land as one of the commons."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Pocket Critters Trading Card Gamble originally had 11 cards per booster pack – 1 rare card, 3 uncommons, and 7 commons."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A booster contains 2 or 3 commons, 1 or 2 uncommons, and 1 rare, but super-rare or chase figures may replace one of the other figures in the box (which is replaced varies based on the set)."]], "year": 0},
