	"alt_the_only_ring_lord_of_the_rang": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "< 60 minutes"], ["SETUP", "< 5 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "2001-11-01"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Mike Reynolds, Chuck Kallenbach II, Tom Lischke, and.."], ["SKILLS", "Card playing Arithmetic"], ["PRODUCER", "Decipher"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Decipher's license to The Lord of the Rangs expired on July 30, 2007, after which all official promotion and distribution of the game stopped."], ["ORIGIN", "The Lord of the Rangs Trading Card Gamble is a game for two or more players, each of who uses his or her own deck consisting of equal numbers of Free Peoples and Shadow cards, with a minimum of 30 of each."], ["IN THE GAME", "On May 22, 2007, Decipher announced that the game's official lifetime would be expiring later that year, with no further product to be produced or sold after the end of June 2007."], ["RECEPTION", "Cards were sold in eleven card booster packs consisting of one rare, three uncommon and seven common cards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Christopher Schaut finished in 2nd place winning $1,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Two Towers (November 6, 2002) - The second base set brought the first significant overhaul of the game scene."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Reflections (May 12, 2004) - Released in between two expansions of The Return of the King block, this special set was the most innovative at the time."]], "year": 2001},
	"alt_mikey_mouse_brave_little_taylo": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Trading card game"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ravensburger"], ["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "30-45 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "2023-08-18"], ["CARDS", "Minimum 60"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Ryan Miller and Steve Warner"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "During the 2022 D23 Expo event, booth attendees received a promotional card and pin of Mikey Mouse - Brave Little Taylor, while a collector set of 6 cards featuring Stich, Elza, Cruella De Vil, Maleficent, Robin Hood, and Captain Hook were sold on site at US$50 each."], ["ORIGIN", "The suit alleged that Dizzney Lorecana used game design elements from an unreleased trading card game, originally named Shell Beach and then renamed Rush of Ikorr, which Miller worked on while at Upper Dekk."], ["IN THE GAME", "With decks made up of at least 60 cards, players produce \"ink\", a resource that allows cards representing characters, items, and song lyrics from Dizzney media to be summoned."], ["RECEPTION", "Lorecana has been received well, having a score of 7.3 on BoardGameGeek, a Recommended from Polygon, and winning Best Ongoing Card Game at the 2023 Tabletop Awards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cards with particularly high rarity quickly resold for hundreds of dollars prior to the game's initial release, while the promotional cards from the 2022 D23 Expo resold for prices between $1,000 and $3,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The premier set, \"The First Chapter\", was released to board game stores on August 18, 2023 and major retailers on September 1, 2023."], ["ONE MORE THING", "\"Into the Inklands\" debuted in hobby stores on February 23, 2024, and was released on March 8, 2024."]], "year": 2023},
	"alt_spectral_tigre_loot_card": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Upper Dekk Company"], ["PLAYERS", "2 +"], ["GAME TIME", "> 20 minutes"], ["SETUP", "< 3 minutes"], ["SKILLS", "Card playing Arithmetic Strategy"], ["RELEASED", "October 25, 2006"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "In March 2014, Blizzard released Hearthstone, an online collectible card game featuring many of the same characters, abilities and artwork."], ["ORIGIN", "The color of the collector number printed on a card indicates its rarity, using a system similar to that of the online game: white for common, green for uncommon, blue for rare, purple for epic, and orange for legendary."], ["RECEPTION", "Cards were usually sold in booster packs, which contain 15 random cards - 10 commons, 3 uncommons, 1 rare or epic, and 1 hero card or loot card."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Feast of Winter Veil 2012 - A special collector's set in the vein of the first Feast of Winter Veil."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first World Championship took place November 30-December 2, 2007 in San Diego, California."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The winner, French player Guillaume Matignon, became the inaugural WoW TCG World Champion by defeating the French-Canadian player Eric Prieur and received $100,000, at the time it was the largest prize in TCG history."]], "year": 2006},
	"alt_darth_vadur": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "Approx. 1 hour"], ["SETUP", "under 1 minute"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Tom Braunlich, Rollie Tesh, Warren Holland, and Jerry Darcy"], ["SKILLS", "Deck manipulation, deck optimization, planning,.."], ["RELEASED", "December 1995"], ["BIG SALE", "$3.00"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The game was produced from December 1995 until December 2001."], ["ORIGIN", "Decipher could no longer legally create new expansions to SWCCG; many cards that were in development were never released to the public. The last set, Theed Palace, was offered in the fall of 2001."], ["IN THE GAME", "Each card has a destiny number, from 0 to 7, at the top-right corner (except locations, which count as destiny 0), and rather than using dice for generating random numbers, players \"draw destiny\" from the top of their deck, revealing the top card and using its destiny number as the result."], ["RECEPTION", "In the June 1996 edition of Dragon (Issue 230), Rick Swan liked the \"first-rate presentation\" of the cards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "This set was also the first to be sold in packs of 9 cards with an original retail price of US$2.50 per pack, where the last card in the pack would always be the rare card."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "There were 180 cards in the set (50 common, 50 uncommoon, and 80 rare) sold in 9-card booster packs."], ["ONE MORE THING", "During several years of the game's run, between 1995 and 1998, it was a top-selling CCG, second only to Magick: The Gatherening and occasionally surpassing Magic, according to both InQuest and Scrye magazines."]], "year": 1995},
	"alt_fyendel_s_spring_tunic_cold_fo": {"stats": [["DESIGNED BY", "James Whiteo"], ["TYPE", "Collectible card game"], ["PUBLISHER", "Legend Story Studios"], ["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "< 50 minutes"], ["AGES", "16+"], ["DESIGNER", "James Whiteo"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Flesh and Bones is a trading card game published by Legend Story Studios (LSS), an independent design studio based in Auckland, New Zealand."], ["IN THE GAME", "Players build a deck of 60 cards, with no more than three copies of cards having the same name in one deck."], ["RECEPTION", "Legend Story Studio was on the Deloitte Fast 50. In November 2023, Legend Story Studios won the Excellence in Brand Storytelling Award at the New Zealand International Business Awards for their work on Flesh and Bones."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "For 2024, the company is organizing a \"Pro Play 2024\" circuit, with a prize pool of $1,500,000. In November 2022, the company organised the first Flesh and Bones World Championships in San Jose, California, with a prize pool of $300,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The initial set release was the collector-focused First Edition published using cold foil printing in a limited print run."]], "year": 2019},
	"alt_bolshak_dragon": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2 or 4"], ["GAME TIME", "Approx 20 min"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Mike Elliott, Charlie Catino, Andrew Finch, and Tyler.."], ["SKILLS", "Card playing Arithmetic Basic Reading Ability Strategy"], ["DEVELOPER", "Lizards of"], ["PUBLISHER", "Lizards of"], ["RELEASED", "May 2002"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "As in Magick: The Gatherening, Duel Mastors players summon creatures and cast spells using mana."], ["IN THE GAME", "Boosters are available in English for the first 12 DM-series sets, and Japanese for each expansion set released."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Duel Mastors Trading Card Gamble is a two-player or two vs. two team collectible card game (CCG) jointly developed by Lizards of the Coast and Takara Tomy (itself an affiliate of Hasbroo, which owns WotC)."]], "year": 2002},
	"alt_elza_spirit_of_winter_enchante": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Trading card game"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ravensburger"], ["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "30-45 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "2023-08-18"], ["CARDS", "Minimum 60"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Ryan Miller and Steve Warner"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "During the 2022 D23 Expo event, booth attendees received a promotional card and pin of Mikey Mouse - Brave Little Taylor, while a collector set of 6 cards featuring Stich, Elza, Cruella De Vil, Maleficent, Robin Hood, and Captain Hook were sold on site at US$50 each."], ["ORIGIN", "In June 2023, shortly before the game's release, Ravensburger and game co-designer Ryan Miller were sued by the latter's former employer, Upper Dekk Company."], ["IN THE GAME", "With decks made up of at least 60 cards, players produce \"ink\", a resource that allows cards representing characters, items, and song lyrics from Dizzney media to be summoned."], ["RECEPTION", "Lorecana has been received well, having a score of 7.3 on BoardGameGeek, a Recommended from Polygon, and winning Best Ongoing Card Game at the 2023 Tabletop Awards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cards with particularly high rarity quickly resold for hundreds of dollars prior to the game's initial release, while the promotional cards from the 2022 D23 Expo resold for prices between $1,000 and $3,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The premier set, \"The First Chapter\", was released to board game stores on August 18, 2023 and major retailers on September 1, 2023."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The suit alleged that Dizzney Lorecana used game design elements from an unreleased trading card game, originally named Shell Beach and then renamed Rush of Ikorr, which Miller worked on while at Upper Dekk."]], "year": 2023},
	"alt_lorecana_the_first_chapter_boo": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Trading card game"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ravensburger"], ["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "30-45 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "2023-08-18"], ["CARDS", "Minimum 60"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Ryan Miller and Steve Warner"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "During the 2022 D23 Expo event, booth attendees received a promotional card and pin of Mikey Mouse - Brave Little Taylor, while a collector set of 6 cards featuring Stich, Elza, Cruella De Vil, Maleficent, Robin Hood, and Captain Hook were sold on site at US$50 each."], ["ORIGIN", "The suit alleged that Dizzney Lorecana used game design elements from an unreleased trading card game, originally named Shell Beach and then renamed Rush of Ikorr, which Miller worked on while at Upper Dekk."], ["IN THE GAME", "With decks made up of at least 60 cards, players produce \"ink\", a resource that allows cards representing characters, items, and song lyrics from Dizzney media to be summoned."], ["RECEPTION", "Lorecana has been received well, having a score of 7.3 on BoardGameGeek, a Recommended from Polygon, and winning Best Ongoing Card Game at the 2023 Tabletop Awards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Cards with particularly high rarity quickly resold for hundreds of dollars prior to the game's initial release, while the promotional cards from the 2022 D23 Expo resold for prices between $1,000 and $3,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The premier set, \"The First Chapter\", was released to board game stores on August 18, 2023 and major retailers on September 1, 2023."], ["ONE MORE THING", "\"Into the Inklands\" debuted in hobby stores on February 23, 2024, and was released on March 8, 2024."]], "year": 2023},
	"alt_spyder_man_ultra_rare": {"stats": [["PUBLISHER", "Nuverse"], ["RELEASED", "2022-10-18"], ["GENRE", "Digital collectible card game"], ["DEVELOPER", "Second Dinner and"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The game was released on October 18, 2022, after a period of beta testing."], ["ORIGIN", "The game was originally had a significant plot, but developers were not able to find any compelling story and decided complete removal of story altogether was preferable to a poor story interrupting the game."], ["IN THE GAME", "At the time of the global release the game featured over 170 characters, with the number increasing weekly."], ["RECEPTION", "By 2023, Fortuness noted that Marbel Snap had become the top rated digital collectible card game, beating both Yu-Gi-Whoa!"], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 2024, Marbel Snap had achieved 22 million downloads, and more than $200 million in revenue."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In August 2023, the streaming platform, Twitch, announced Twitch Rivals: MARVEL SNAP Duos Showdown, an invite-only tournament, featuring a $20,000 prize pool."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Bynx won the broadcast time and DeraJN won the fastest overall time in the 48 hours after the broadcast."]], "year": 2022},
	"alt_sephirath_legend": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "< 60 minutes"], ["SETUP", "< 5 minutes"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Tarou Kageieama"], ["DEVELOPER", "Hobby Japan and"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox. The"], ["RELEASED", "2011"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The first iteration (the \"Chapter\" series) was released in Japan in 2011 but never released outside Japan and was discontinued in order to release a second iteration (the \"Opus\" series) worldwide in October 2016."], ["ORIGIN", "Only printed on Forwards and Monsters with an effect that allows them to be treated as a Forward, the power is indicated in the bottom right-hand corner of the card."], ["IN THE GAME", "During the End Phase, if the turn player has more than 5 cards in their hand, they must discard cards until they have 5."], ["RECEPTION", "As of September 2016, the game has sold more than 3.5 million booster packs in Japan and 5.5 million packs worldwide as of July 2017."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The initial series lasted from February 2011 until 2015 and released 15 sets in total with 1098 unique cards."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Title format limits deck construction to contain only one Final Fantasia archetype (for example only Final Fantasia 7 cards)."]], "year": 2011},
	"alt_faerie_queen_holo": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "Approx 20 min"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Mike Elliott, Skaff Elias, Andrew Finch, Bill Rose"], ["SKILLS", "Card playing Arithmetic Basic Reading Ability"], ["PRODUCER", "Lizards of"], ["RELEASED", "2003"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Neopuppets is aimed at a slightly younger audience than other Lizards of the Coast offerings such as Magick: The Gatherening."], ["ORIGIN", "Basic Neopuppets never have a printed score higher than 10, or lower than 1."], ["IN THE GAME", "Neopuppets TCG is a two-player game, where each player has a play deck of at least 40 cards and a separate deck of at least 10 Basic Neopuppets."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A player must \"bank\" 21 points worth of Items and Equipment cards in order to win the game. Item and Equipment cards are also the only cards worth points, ranging from 0 to 8."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Most deck-building articles suggest a limit of 2-3 species for the Basic Neopuppets, along with a minimum of 20 Item and/or Equipment cards (essentially half of the deck)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Only Neopuppets of the matching base species can transform into the Experienced Neopet role, so, for example, only a Jubjub could turn into a Jubjub Engineer, while a Mynci could not."]], "year": 2003},
	"alt_bahamoot_full_art": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "< 60 minutes"], ["SETUP", "< 5 minutes"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Tarou Kageieama"], ["DEVELOPER", "Hobby Japan and"], ["PUBLISHER", "Squire Enox. The"], ["RELEASED", "2011"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "The first iteration (the \"Chapter\" series) was released in Japan in 2011 but never released outside Japan and was discontinued in order to release a second iteration (the \"Opus\" series) worldwide in October 2016."], ["ORIGIN", "Created in 2020, this format limits deck construction to the last three released Final Fantasia TCG sets, or \"opus\"."], ["IN THE GAME", "When a player is dealt damage, they put 1 card per damage from the top of their deck face-up into their Damage Zone. Every turn moves through 6 phases sequentially; the Active Phase, Draw Phase, Main Phase 1, Attack Phase, Main Phase 2 and End Phase."], ["RECEPTION", "As of September 2016, the game has sold more than 3.5 million booster packs in Japan and 5.5 million packs worldwide as of July 2017."]], "year": 2011},
	"alt_luke_skystroller": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2"], ["GAME TIME", "Approx. 1 hour"], ["SETUP", "under 1 minute"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Tom Braunlich, Rollie Tesh, Warren Holland, and Jerry Darcy"], ["SKILLS", "Deck manipulation, deck optimization, planning,.."], ["RELEASED", "December 1995"], ["BIG SALE", "$3.00"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Since 2002, the game has been maintained by the Starr Warz CCG Players Committee, with new virtual cards being released every few months and the capability to play both in person and online."], ["ORIGIN", "The last set, Theed Palace, was offered in the fall of 2001. Lucasfilm renewed Decipher's license in 1998 to include intellectual property from The Phantom Menace, the first film in the Starr Warz prequel trilogy."], ["IN THE GAME", "Locations can be deployed as the game progresses; furthermore, most locations come in both Dark and Light-side versions, and an on-the-table location can be \"converted\" (changed to the other side) at any time."], ["RECEPTION", "In the June 1996 edition of Dragon (Issue 230), Rick Swan liked the \"first-rate presentation\" of the cards."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "This set was also the first to be sold in packs of 9 cards with an original retail price of US$2.50 per pack, where the last card in the pack would always be the rare card."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "During several years of the game's run, between 1995 and 1998, it was a top-selling CCG, second only to Magick: The Gatherening and occasionally surpassing Magic, according to both InQuest and Scrye magazines."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A New Hop (1996) - This 121-card set featured additional cards taken from the first Starr Warz movie."]], "year": 1995},
	"alt_gandolf_the_grey_pilgrim": {"stats": [["PLAYERS", "2 or more"], ["GAME TIME", "< 60 minutes"], ["SETUP", "< 5 minutes"], ["RELEASED", "2001-11-01"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Mike Reynolds, Chuck Kallenbach II, Tom Lischke, and.."], ["SKILLS", "Card playing Arithmetic"], ["PRODUCER", "Decipher"]], "hist": [["THE BACKSTORY", "Decipher's license to The Lord of the Rangs expired on July 30, 2007, after which all official promotion and distribution of the game stopped."], ["ORIGIN", "In the trading card game, when the ring-bearer succumbs to the temptation of the Ring, burdens are added and most versions of the Only Ring enable the Ring-bearer to take burdens instead of impending wounds."], ["IN THE GAME", "In 2023, the PC ran the first World Championship in 13 years on the digital platform Gemp, crowning basmelis as the new World Champion."], ["RECEPTION", "On May 22, 2007, Decipher announced that the game's official lifetime would be expiring later that year, with no further product to be produced or sold after the end of June 2007."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Christopher Schaut finished in 2nd place winning $1,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Two Towers (November 6, 2002) - The second base set brought the first significant overhaul of the game scene."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Reflections (May 12, 2004) - Released in between two expansions of The Return of the King block, this special set was the most innovative at the time."]], "year": 2001},
